==Trivia==
*Urs is the rider, Fenrir is the wolf.
*The name Urs (ウルス ''Urusu'') may be derived from ウルズ ''Uruzu'' ([[wikipedia:Urðr|Urðr]], one of the three goddesses of fate in Norse mythology), ''urus'' (Malay and Indonesian for "to manage"), ''uruş'' (Turkmen for war), or the feminine given name ''Ursula''. Where the other [[wikipedia:Verðandi|Verðandi]] and [[wikipedia:Skuld|Skuld]] represent the present and future, respectively, Urðr represents the past, correlating with [[Urs & Fenrir (Enemy)|enemy Urs & Fenrir]]'s [[:Category:Relic Enemies|Relic]] trait; the three [[wikipedia:Norns|Norns]] live beneath and tend to the world tree, [[wikipedia:Yggdrasil|Yggdrasil]], which may be represented by the [[Media:Bg1028.gif|huge, glowing tree]] in the last four [[Uncanny Legends]] stages.
**It may also be derived from ''c<u>urs</u>e'', though this is less likely, as ''curse'' transliterated into Japanese is カース ''kāsu''.
*In Norse mythology, [[wikipedia:Fenrir|Fenrir]] is a monstrous wolf and child of Loki and a giantess, Angrboða.
*Minka's Japanese name, Mike, means "calico fur" in Japanese; it can also be short for ''mikeneko'', meaning "calico cat".
*Urs is potentially an ancestor of [[Ururun Wolf (Special Cat)|Ururun]], as referred to in the ending of [[Legend Stages|Stories of Legend]].
*Urs & Fenrir, Ururun Wolf, [[Masked Yulala (Special Cat)|Masked Yulala]] and [[Master Uril (Special Cat)|Master Uril]] have many similarities:
**Counting Yulala's Japanese name (Urara), their names all start with "Ur".
**Their Japanese descriptions all start with a quote followed by how they joined the Cat Army, which always includes the word 勝手 (''katte'', meaning "selfish", "self-serving" or "acting on one's own without asking").
**Their attack animations all include electricity.
**All have anti-[[:Category:Relic Enemies|Relic]] abilities and immunity to Curse in at least one of their forms.

